# TypeScript Coding Style Rule

Follow the spirit of Effective Go and Go Code Review Comments, adapted to this TypeScript codebase.

## 1) Code shape
- Prefer small, focused functions or hooks with one clear responsibility.
- Keep control flow straightforward; prefer early returns over deep nesting.
- Use explicit types when inference would hide intent at a boundary or shared seam.
- Name things by responsibility instead of implementation detail.
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions for single call sites unless they clarify a real boundary.

## 2) Components, services, and utilities
- Keep components focused on rendering and local interaction; move non-trivial playback, scheduling, or persistence logic into services, hooks, or helpers.
- Keep services narrow and purpose-driven instead of accumulating unrelated responsibilities.
- Prefer helpers that can be exercised without browser globals when the behavior does not truly need them.

## 3) Comment philosophy
- Prefer more comment context than usual when logic is non-obvious or likely to be amended later by another agentic coding session.
- Comments should act as durable engineering memory for future amendments.
- Store design intent, trade-offs, constraints, invariants, failure modes, and operational assumptions in comments when they matter to safe modification.
- If a simpler-looking implementation was intentionally avoided, say why.
- Do not add comments that merely restate what the code already says.

## 4) Preferred posture for future-friendly code
- Optimize for safe amendment by future contributors and agents, not for cleverness.
- When behavior depends on timing, media loading, browser quirks, persisted recovery, or device command ordering, leave enough context in comments for the next session to reason safely.
- If a TODO remains, it must be specific and actionable; use `TBD by repo owner` only for unresolved top-level architecture or API policy.
